# FSCK(1M)

## NAME

fsck - file system consistency check and interactive repair

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/fsck** [ **-y** ] [ **-n** ] [ **-s** ] [ **-S** ] *filesystem ...*

## DESCRIPTION

*Fsck* audits and interactively repairs inconsistent conditions for the named *filesystems*. If
a file system is consistent then the number of files, number of blocks used, and number of
blocks free are reported. If the file system is inconsistent the operator is prompted for
concurrence before each correction is attempted. Most corrections lose data; all losses are
reported. The default action for each correction is to wait for the operator to respond `yes`
or `no`. Without write permission *fsck* defaults to **-n** action.

These options are recognized:

**-y**
: Assume a yes response to all questions.

**-n**
: Assume a no response to all questions.

**-s**
: Ignore the actual free list and (unconditionally) construct a new one by rewriting the
  super-block of the file system. The file system should be unmounted while this is done, or
  extreme care should be taken that the system is quiescent and that it is rebooted immediately
  afterwards. This precaution is necessary so that the old, bad, in-core copy of the superblock
  will not continue to be used, or written on the file system.

> **Note:** v7's **-s** took an argument (*X*) describing how to interleave the new free list -
> **-s3**, **-s4** or **-s**\&*c*\&**:**\&*s* - and this port's takes none. The interleave was a
> rotational optimization for a moving-head pack, described by the *s_m* and *s_n* fields of the
> super-block; neither field exists in this filesystem (see fs(5)), the disk is not such a pack,
> and *mkfs*(1M) builds a plain descending free list. So does this.

**-S**
: Conditionally reconstruct the free list. This option is like **-s** except that the free list
  is rebuilt only if there were no discrepancies discovered in the file system. It is useful for
  forcing free list reorganization on uncontaminated file systems. **-S** forces **-n**.

**Note:** v7's **-t** option, which named a scratch file for *fsck*'s tables when memory was
short, is not implemented and is not needed. The tables are allocated from the heap and sized
from the super-block; on a filesystem filling one drive they come to about eight kilobytes.

**Note:** v7 with no *filesystem* argument read a default list from **/etc/checklist**. There
is no such file on this system and one filesystem to name, so an argument is required.

## BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES

A filesystem block here is **3072** bytes, and every count *fsck* reports of used or free space
is in **1024-byte** blocks - three of them to the block, as *df*(1M), du(1) and *quot*(1M) also
report. Two consequences: every such number is a multiple of three, and it is *half* what a
PDP-11 would have printed rather than a sixth, v7 having counted 512-byte blocks.

A block *number* is not a measurement and is not converted. `119 DUP I=8` names block 119 of
the volume, which is where dd(1) and *icheck*(1M) would look for it. Nor is a count of
occurrences: `3 BAD BLKS IN FREE LIST` is three entries, not three kilobytes.

## THE RAW DEVICE

Checking the raw device is almost always faster, and on this system it is also the only way to
check a mounted filesystem, there being no ustat(2). A transfer through **/dev/rmd0** is
subject to four conditions *fsck* obeys and a v7 program knows nothing about: the buffer must
begin at byte 0 of a word, the count must be a whole number of blocks, the buffer's word
address must be a multiple of 512, and the seek offset must be block-aligned. Every read and
every write this program makes is one whole block into one of four buffers it aligns at
startup.

A repair adds a fifth: a raw *write* may not be sourced from the text segment. No buffer here
is.

## SUPER-USER ONLY

**/dev/rmd0** is mode 0600, because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
*Fsck* is not set-user-id and must not become so: it *writes* that node.

It is also read - and repaired - through the same node the running system is using. If *fsck*
modifies a *mounted* filesystem it prints

> \*\*\*\*\* BOOT UNIX (NO SYNC!) \*\*\*\*\*

and does not return, since the kernel's in-core super-block is now older than the disk's and
letting it be written back would undo the repair. The machine must be halted rather than shut
down.

## WHAT IS CHECKED

Inconsistencies checked are as follows:

1.
: Blocks claimed by more than one inode or the free list.

2.
: Blocks claimed by an inode or the free list outside the range of the file system.

3.
: Incorrect link counts.

4.
: Size checks:

  > Directory size not a multiple of 24 bytes.

5.
: Bad inode format.

6.
: Blocks not accounted for anywhere.

7.
: Directory checks:

  > | File pointing to unallocated inode.
  > | Inode number out of range.

8.
: Super Block checks:

  > | Not a filesystem (bad magic number).
  > | A geometry this system cannot read: block size, inodes per block or addresses per inode other than its own.
  > | More blocks for inodes than there are in the file system.

9.
: Bad free block list format.

10.
: Total free block and/or free inode count incorrect.

**Note:** the two super-block counters the tenth names, *s_tfree* and *s_tinode*, were
maintained by nothing in this system until the kernel took them up, and while that was so
*fsck* could only report a discrepancy as a note: a repair would have been undone by the next
write. Both are now kept by the kernel's block and i-node allocator as blocks and i-nodes are
allocated and freed, so a wrong one is a real inconsistency and is offered as a **FIX**. The
free inode count excludes i-node 1, which exists and can never be allocated; see filsys(5).

Orphaned files and directories (allocated but unreferenced) are, with the operator's
concurrence, reconnected by placing them in the **lost+found** directory. The name assigned is
the inode number. The only restriction is that **lost+found** must preexist in the root of the
filesystem being checked.

**Note:** v7 added that it must also `have empty slots in which entries can be made`, obtained
by creating files in it and deleting them again. That is not true here. *Fsck* rounds the
directory's size up to a whole block before it looks, a block holds 128 entries, and everything
past **.** and **..** is zero — so a **lost+found** made by mkdir(1) and never touched has
126 free slots.

## DIAGNOSTICS

**Can't stat** *filesystem*
: The argument does not exist.

**Can't open** *filesystem*
: It exists and could not be opened for reading.

*filesystem*\&**: not a filesystem**
: The super-block's magic number is wrong. Nothing is checked.

*filesystem*\&**: filesystem geometry mismatch**
: It is a filesystem, made by a system whose block size, inodes per block or addresses per inode
  differ from this one's. The kernel would refuse to mount it for the same reason; see fs(5).

**Size check: fsize** *n* **isize** *m*
: The super-block's two sizes are not consistent with each other.

**Can't get memory**
: The tables do not fit. On a filesystem this system can address they always do.

**CAN NOT READ: BLK** *n*
: An I/O error. On the raw device this most often means the transfer broke one of the conditions
  above rather than that the disk is bad.

**Incompatible options: -n and -s**
: Salvaging the free list is a write.

## FILES

/lost+found
: directory for connecting files with no directory entry

## SEE ALSO

dcheck(1M), icheck(1M), mkfs(1M), df(1M), fs(5)

## BUGS

- Inode numbers for **.** and **..** in each directory should be checked for validity.
- The **-b** option of *icheck*(1M) should be available.
- A second mounted filesystem is not recognized as mounted: only the root is, and only because
  its name can be derived. There is no ustat(2) and, so far, nothing else to mount.

## EXIT STATUS

0 if every filesystem named was examined, 4 if interrupted, 8 on a fatal error. A filesystem
that could not be opened or was not a filesystem is reported and skipped; as in v7, that alone
does not change the exit status.
